<BaseLayout title="Bioelectronics — Mishti Broor">
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">Bioelectronics</div><h1>Where biology meets hardware.</h1><p>I’m building toward technologies that can sense, interpret, and eventually interact with physiological and neural systems outside controlled laboratory environments.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container"><div class="grid">
    <div class="card span-6"><div class="eyebrow">Neural interfaces</div><h3>Recording & stimulation</h3><p>EEG, ear-EEG, neural recording, BCI systems, and neuromodulation.</p></div>
    <div class="card span-6"><div class="eyebrow">Wearable biosensing</div><h3>Signals in everyday life</h3><p>ECG, EMG, PPG, EDA, biochemical sensing, and multimodal physiological systems.</p></div>
    <div class="card span-6"><div class="eyebrow">Hardware</div><h3>From electrode to embedded system</h3><p>Electrodes, analog front ends, low-noise amplification, filtering, PCBs, and embedded electronics.</p></div>
    <div class="card span-6"><div class="eyebrow">Human-centered devices</div><h3>Designed for variability</h3><p>Human studies, clinical validation, signal quality, usability, and real-world deployment.</p></div>
  </div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container prose"><h2>Why this direction</h2><p>My EEG electrode work made one thing clear: measuring a biological signal is not only a signal-processing problem. The interface between the body and the device matters just as much. That is what draws me to bioelectronics — the intersection of materials, electronics, sensing, human physiology, and device design.</p><h2>What I’m building next</h2><p>This page is intentionally a living notebook. As I build experience in analog electronics, PCB design, embedded systems, and wearable sensing, I’ll document small experiments here rather than waiting until they become polished “projects.”</p></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
